# What copy-db.sh changes in the copy of the live database before the dev server gets it (chapter 14).
# Copy this file to scrub.list beside copy-db.sh and write your own rules: one per line, table, column, what to do.
#
#   blank       the text becomes empty ('')            for text columns that may not be NULL
#   null        the value becomes NULL                 for any column that allows NULL
#   pseudonym   the text becomes p + 12 letters/digits  the same name gives the same pseudonym everywhere in one
#                                                       copy, so tables that join on it still join; the secret
#                                                       that makes them is new each copy and never kept
#   empty       every row of the table goes            write * as the column; for logs and anything you'd rather
#                                                       builders never see
#   keep        nothing; says you looked               for a column whose name looks private but isn't
#
# copy-db.sh stops, and the dev server keeps the copy it had, if a rule names a table or column that doesn't
# exist, or if any column whose name looks private (ip, cdkey, password, email, discord, token, secret...) has no
# rule. So when a migration adds such a column, the next copy asks you about it instead of passing it on.
#
# These lines are an example for a world with an accounts table and a chat log. Your tables will differ.
#
# table        column          what
# players      account         pseudonym
# players      cdkey           blank
# players      ip_address      null
# players      password_hash   null
# players      discord_id      null
# characters   account         pseudonym
# chat_log     *               empty
# settings     api_token_name  keep
